d/choices" "value" [["math" "b=-1,\\,c=-1"] ["math" "b=-1,\\,c=1"] ["math" "b=1,\\,c=-1"] ["math" "b=1,\\,c=0"] ["math" "b=1,\\,c=1"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "4fb8e5c4946e737c7fded9b0e532d60c1cb37f6fb365cfeb6698aae22306fb1b" "rationale" "At x=1 the left limit is 3+b and the right limit is 3+c. Setting each equal to 2 gives b=c=-1, original option 0. Stored text 'b = $-1$; c = $-1$' expresses the same result. Preserve the authentic option's math type. This is a source-backed derived judgment."}] "usage" {"q-21026" [] "q-119865" [] "q-125550" [] "q-73432" [] "q-115511" [] "q-75133" [] "q-65502" [] "q-73670" [] "q-71779" [] "q-112044" [] "q-113944" [] "q-114961" [] "q-78257" [] "q-141281" [] "q-40081" [] "q-36743" [] "q-73306" [] "q-41944" []} "basis" 1236 "retractions" [[17592186257386 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1$"] [17592186257386 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}x={sin}^{2}x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186257386 :question/answer-fields 17592186274359] [17592186258090 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$"] [17592186258090 :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186258090 :question/answer-fields 17592186274463] [17592186260869 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$"] [17592186260869 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186260869 :question/answer-fields 17592186274816] [17592186248072 :question/problem "$\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x-2csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186248072 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-csc⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =tan⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x-2csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =tan⁡x-2(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =tan⁡x+2csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186256857 :question/answer-fields 17592186274243] [17592186257116 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186257116 :question/answer-fields 17592186274310] [17592186257802 :question/answer-fields 17592186274431] [17592186261704 :question/answer-fields 17592186275019] [17592186261574 :question/answer-fields 17592186261575] [17592186261501 :question/answer-fields 17592186274968] [17592186257134 :question/answer-fields 17592186274314] [17592186260204 :question/answer-fields 17592186274668] [17592186258842 :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{'}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$"] [17592186258842 :question/answer-fields 17592186274560] [17592186261529 :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u={2}^{z}-9.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}={2}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\,\\text{d}u={2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\cdot {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\i